"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have completely solved the problem.  \nFor an odd integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nis attainable.  \nFor an even integer \\(n\\ge 4\\) every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\n\\]\n\nis attainable, while \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n*Notation.*  \nFor a positive integer \\(n\\) let  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\n(the set of lattice points that have to be covered).  \n\\(|T_n|= \\displaystyle\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\n*Lemma 1.*  \nA sunny line contains at most \\(\\displaystyle\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the line as \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nIf \\(m\\) is rational, say \\(m=p/q\\) in lowest terms, then the integer points on the line form an arithmetic progression with step \\((q,p)\\).  \nInside the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) at most one point of the progression can have a given value of \\(a+b\\); the possible sums range from \\(2\\) to \\(n+1\\), i.e. \\(n\\) different values.  \nBecause the step in the direction \\((1,1)\\) is non\u2011zero (otherwise the line would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)), the progression meets at most every second sum, whence at most \\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\) points.  \u220e\n\n\n\n*Corollary 1.*  \nIf \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny, then the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered by the sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe remaining points must be covered by the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \nA non\u2011sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (e.g. the vertical line \\(x=1\\)).  \nHence the total number of points that can be covered by **any** configuration of \\(n\\) lines is at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor+(n-k)n .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince all \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) points have to be covered, (2) must be at least \\(|T_n|\\).  \nFrom this inequality we obtain\n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn, & n\\ \\text{odd},\\\\[2mm]\nn-1, & n\\ \\text{even}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus for even \\(n\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is impossible, while for odd \\(n\\) no restriction on \\(k\\) follows from the counting argument.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Constructions attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nWe now show that the bounds in (3) are sharp.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1. The case \\(n\\) odd  \n\nWrite \\(n=2t+1\\) (\\(t\\ge 1\\)).  \nConsider the \\(n\\) **sunny** lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = x - i\\qquad (i=0,1,\\dots ,2t).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEach line has slope \\(1\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore it is sunny.  \nFor a fixed \\(i\\) the integer points of \\(T_n\\) on \\(L_i\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\{(a,a-i)\\mid a\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;1\\le a\\le t+1,\\;a-i\\ge 1\\bigr\\},\n\\]\n\nhence exactly \\(t+1=\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) points.  \nThe sets of points belonging to different \\(L_i\\) are disjoint, and their union is precisely \\(T_n\\).  \nConsequently the family \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=0}^{2t}\\) gives a configuration with **\\(k=n\\)** sunny lines.\n\nTo obtain a configuration with **any prescribed** number \\(k\\) of sunny lines (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)) we proceed as follows.\n\n* Choose any \\(k\\) of the lines in (4); they remain sunny.\n* Replace each of the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines by a distinct **non\u2011sunny** line that passes through at least one point already covered by the chosen sunny lines (for instance a vertical line \\(x=a\\) or a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\)).  \n  Because the sunny lines already cover all points of \\(T_n\\), adding such non\u2011sunny lines does not destroy the covering property, and the total number of lines stays \\(n\\).\n\nThus for odd \\(n\\) every integer \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2. The case \\(n\\) even  \n\nWrite \\(n=2t\\) (\\(t\\ge 2\\)).  \nWe first construct a configuration with **\\(k=n-1\\)** sunny lines and one non\u2011sunny line.\n\n*Non\u2011sunny line.*  \nTake the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV:\\;x=1 .\n\\]\n\nIt contains exactly \\(n=2t\\) points of \\(T_n\\):\n\\((1,1),(1,2),\\dots ,(1,2t)\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nFor each \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,2t-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_j:\\; y = x - j .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAll \\(S_j\\) have slope \\(1\\) and are therefore sunny.  \nFor a fixed \\(j\\) the points of \\(T_n\\) on \\(S_j\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\{(a,a-j)\\mid a\\in\\mathbb Z,\\; \\max\\{1,j+1\\}\\le a\\le t+1\\bigr\\},\n\\]\n\nhence exactly \\(t\\) points.  \nThe sets \\(\\{S_j\\cap T_n\\}_{j=1}^{2t-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint, and together with the points of \\(V\\) they partition \\(T_n\\).  \nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C=\\{V\\}\\cup\\{S_j\\mid 1\\le j\\le 2t-1\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(n-1\\) of which are sunny, and it covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Obtaining fewer sunny lines.*  \nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \nSelect arbitrarily \\(k\\) of the sunny lines \\(S_j\\) and keep them sunny.  \nReplace each of the remaining \\(n-1-k\\) sunny lines by a distinct non\u2011sunny line (again vertical or diagonal) that passes through points already covered by the retained sunny lines.  \nBecause the original family \\(\\mathcal C\\) already covers all points, the new family still covers \\(T_n\\).  \nThus we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for every \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for even \\(n\\)\n\nAssume \\(n=2t\\) and that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 each line can contain at most \\(t\\) points of \\(T_n\\); hence the total number of points that can be covered is at most \\(nt = 2t^{2}\\).  \nBut  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 = \\frac{2t(2t+1)}2 = 2t^{2}+t > 2t^{2},\n\\]\n\na contradiction.  Therefore a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines does **not** exist when \\(n\\) is even.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \n\n* If \\(n\\) is odd, every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realised.  \n* If \\(n\\) is even, every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) can be realised, and \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line contains at most \\(\\displaystyle\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the line as \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nIf \\(m\\) is irrational the line contains at most one lattice point, so the bound holds.  \nAssume \\(m=p/q\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) and \\(q>0\\).  \nAll integer points on the line are of the form  \n\n\\[\n(a,b)=(a_0+qk,\\;b_0+pk),\\qquad k\\in\\mathbb Z,\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nfor some particular solution \\((a_0,b_0)\\).  \nThe quantity \\(a+b\\) changes by \\(q+p\\) when \\(k\\) increases by \\(1\\).  \nBecause the line is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), we have \\(p+q\\neq 0\\); consequently the values of \\(a+b\\) taken by the points of the progression are all distinct modulo \\(|p+q|\\).  \n\nInside the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) the possible sums are the integers \\(2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\) \u2013 exactly \\(n\\) values.  \nSince two consecutive points of the progression differ by \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\) (otherwise the slope would be \\(-1\\) or \\(0\\)), at most every second sum can be realised.  Hence at most \\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\) points of the progression belong to \\(T_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Corollary 1  \nIf among the \\(n\\) lines exactly \\(k\\) are sunny, then the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered by the sunny lines does not exceed  \n\n\\[\nk\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nA non\u2011sunny line (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (the vertical line \\(x=1\\) attains this bound).  \nHence the whole family of \\(n\\) lines can cover at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor+(n-k)n\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\npoints.  \nSince all \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) points must be covered, (9) must be at least \\(|T_n|\\).  This yields\n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn, & n\\ \\text{odd},\\\\[2mm]\nn-1, & n\\ \\text{even}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThus for even \\(n\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is impossible; for odd \\(n\\) (10) imposes no restriction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction for odd \\(n\\)\n\nLet \\(n=2t+1\\) (\\(t\\ge 1\\)).  \nConsider the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = x - i\\qquad (i=0,1,\\dots ,2t).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nAll have slope \\(1\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore they are sunny.  \nFor a fixed \\(i\\) the points of \\(T_n\\) on \\(L_i\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,a-i)\\mid a\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;1\\le a\\le t+1,\\;a-i\\ge 1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich contains exactly \\(t+1=\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) points.  \nThe sets \