ines by a distinct non\u2011sunny line (again vertical or diagonal) that passes through points already covered by the retained sunny lines.  \nSince the original family \\(\\mathcal C\\) already covers all points, the new family still does, and it contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nThus every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is attainable for even \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for even \\(n\\)\n\nAssume \\(n=2t\\) and that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 each line can contain at most \\(t\\) points of \\(T_n\\); therefore the total number of points that can be covered is at most \\(nt = 2t^{2}\\).  \nBut  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 = \\frac{2t(2t+1)}2 = 2t^{2}+t > 2t^{2},\n\\]\n\nso the required covering is impossible.  Hence \\(k=n\\) cannot occur when \\(n\\) is even.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer.**  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{array}{ll}\n\\text{If }n\\text{ is odd:}& k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\};\\\\[2mm]\n\\text{If }n\\text{ is even:}& k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\end{array}}\n\\]"
