 \\]\n\n   points (the non\u2011sunny lines can contain at most \\(n\\) points each).  \n   Requiring this to be at least \\(|T_n|\\) yields inequality (1).\n\n3. **Construction for odd \\(n\\).**  \n   Write \\(n=2t+1\\).  \n   For each integer \\(d\\) with \\(-t\\le d\\le t\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_d:\\; y = x - d .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   All these lines have slope \\(1\\) (hence are sunny) and are distinct.  \n   A point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a-b=d\\) for exactly one \\(d\\) in the above range, so it lies on the corresponding line \\(L_d\\).  \n   Thus the family \\(\\{L_d\\mid -t\\le d\\le t\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) sunny lines covering every point of \\(T_n\\).  \n   To obtain a configuration with any prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)), keep any \\(k\\) of the lines \\(L_d\\) and replace each of the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines by an arbitrary non\u2011sunny line (e.g. a vertical line) that passes through at least one point already covered; the covering property is preserved.\n\n4. **Even \\(n\\): impossibility of \\(k=n\\).**  \n   When \\(n=2t\\) is even, Lemma\u202f1 gives the bound \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor = t\\).  \n   If all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, they could cover at most \\(nt = 2t^{2}\\) points, but \\(|T_n| = \\frac{n(n+1)}2 = 2t^{2}+t\\).  \n   Hence a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n5. **Partial construction for even \\(n\\).**  \n   I have found a configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines (the bound in (1) is attained) for several small even values of \\(n\\) (e.g. \\(n=4,6\\)).  \n   The idea is to use one non\u2011sunny line (for instance the vertical line \\(x=1\\)) to cover all points with abscissa \\(1\\), and to cover the remaining points by the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y = x - d\\qquad (d\\in D),\n   \\]\n\n   where the index set \\(D\\) is chosen appropriately for each even \\(n\\).  \n   A complete, uniform description of \\(D\\) that works for every even \\(n\\) and a rigorous verification that the resulting family indeed covers all points of \\(T_n\\) have not yet been finalized.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n*Lemma\u202f1.*  \nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line, i.e. its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap T_n|\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the slope of \\(L\\) is irrational, \\(L\\) contains at most one lattice point, and the inequality holds because \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\ge 1\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nAssume the slope is rational, write it as \\(m=p/q\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) and \\(q>0\\).  \nAll integer points of \\(L\\) are of the form  \n\n\\[\n(a,b)=(a_0+qk,\\;b_0+pk),\\qquad k\\in\\mathbb Z,\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nfor some particular solution \\((a_0,b_0)\\).  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n(a+b)=(a_0+b_0)+(p+q)k .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(L\\) is sunny we have \\(p\\neq0\\) (otherwise the slope would be \\(0\\)), \\(q\\neq0\\) (otherwise the slope would be \\(\\infty\\)), and \\(p+q\\neq0\\) (otherwise the slope would be \\(-1\\)).  \nHence \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\).\n\nThe admissible values of \\(a+b\\) for points of \\(T_n\\) are the integers \\(2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\) \u2013 exactly \\(n\\) distinct numbers.  \nEquation (6) shows that as \\(k\\) varies, the quantity \\(a+b\\) changes by steps of size \\(|p+q|\\ge2\\); therefore at most every second integer in \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) can be realised.  \nThus the number of points of \\(T_n\\) lying on \\(L\\) does not exceed \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n*Corollary 1 (upper bound for sunny lines).*  \nLet a configuration consist of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.  \nA sunny line can contain at most \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (Lemma\u202f1); a non\u2011sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (e.g. the vertical line \\(x=1\\) attains this bound).  \nHence the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor+(n-k)n .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nSince all \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) points must be covered, (7) yields  \n\n\\[\nk\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor+(n-k)n\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSolving (8) gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn, & n\\ \\text{odd},\\\\[2mm]\nn-1, & n\\ \\text{even}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus (9) is a necessary condition for the existence of a configuration.\n\n\n\n*Construction for odd \\(n\\).*  \nLet \\(n=2t+1\\) (\\(t\\ge1\\)).  \nFor each integer \\(d\\) with \\(-t\\le d\\le t\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_d:\\; y = x - d .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nAll lines \\(L_d\\) have slope \\(1\\), hence are sunny, and they are distinct because their intercepts differ.  \n\nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \nSince \\(a+b\\le 2t+2\\) and \\(a,b\\ge1\\), the difference \\(d:=a-b\\) satisfies \\(-t\\le d\\le t\\).  \nConsequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_d\\).  \nThus the family \\(\\{L_d\\mid -t\\le d\\le t\\}\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines and covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nTo obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)):\n\n* keep any \\(k\\) of the lines \\(L_d\\);\n* for each of the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines choose an arbitrary non\u2011sunny line (for instance a vertical line \\(x=c\\) with a suitable integer \\(c\\)) that passes through at least one point already covered by the retained sunny lines.  \n\nBecause the retained sunny lines already cover all points of \\(T_n\\), adding such non\u2011sunny lines does not destroy the covering property, and the total number of lines stays \\(n\\).  \nHence for every odd \\(n\\) and every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n\n\n*Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for even \\(n\\).*  \nLet \\(n=2t\\) be even.  \nIf all \