"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have completely solved the problem.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the statement and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines; and no other value of \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n*Notation.*  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to be covered.  \nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\nThe construction works for an arbitrary integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. **Step\u202f1 \u2013 non\u2011sunny covering of a large part.**  \n   Put \\(t=n-k\\).  \n   Use the \\(t\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=t\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   (these lines are not sunny).  \n   They cover all points of \\(S_n\\) whose second coordinate is \\(\\le t\\).\n\n2. **Step\u202f2 \u2013 the remaining triangle.**  \n   The points that are still uncovered form the right\u2011angled triangle  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid b>t\\}\n        =\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le k,\\;t+1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The triangle has side length \\(k\\) and contains \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points.\n\n3. **Step\u202f3 \u2013 covering the triangle with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.**  \n\n   * (i) **One sunny line of slope\u202f1.**  \n       The points  \n\n       \\[\n       P_i=(i,t+i)\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,k)\n       \\tag{3}\n       \\]\n\n       lie on the line  \n\n       \\[\n       L_0:\\;x-y=t .\n       \\]\n\n       Its slope is \\(1\\); hence \\(L_0\\) is sunny and it covers the \\(k\\) points\n       \\((1,t+1),(2,t+2),\\dots ,(k,n)\\).\n\n   * (ii) **\\(k-1\\) further sunny lines, each covering one remaining point.**  \n       After removing the points of (3) from \\(T_k\\) we are left with the\n       \\(k-1\\) points  \n\n       \\[\n       Q_i=(i,t+1)\\qquad(i=2,3,\\dots ,k).\n       \\tag{4}\n       \\]\n\n       For each \\(i\\) choose a line of slope \\(2\\) passing through \\(Q_i\\); e.g.\n\n       \\[\n       L_i:\\;y- (t+1)=2\\bigl(x-i\\bigr)\\qquad(i=2,\\dots ,k).\n       \\]\n\n       The slope \\(2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n       Because the intercepts \\(2(-i)-(t+1)\\) are distinct, the lines\n       \\(L_2,\\dots ,L_k\\) are pairwise distinct and none of them coincides with\n       \\(L_0\\).\n\n   Altogether we have used exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   1+(k-1)=k\n   \\]\n\n   sunny lines, and together with the \\(t=n-k\\) horizontal lines we have\n   \\(n\\) distinct lines covering every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n4. **Step\u202f4 \u2013 the extreme cases.**  \n\n   * \\(k=0\\): we keep only the horizontal lines (1); all of them are non\u2011sunny.  \n   * \\(k=n\\): we take \\(t=0\\) in the construction above; the triangle\n     \\(T_n=S_n\\) is covered by the line of slope\u202f1 through \\((1,1),(2,2),\\dots\\)\n     and by \\(n-1\\) additional sunny lines, giving exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) a configuration with precisely \\(k\\)\nsunny lines exists.\n\nSince a sunny line is defined only by its slope, any line that is not parallel\nto the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) is admissible; the\nconstruction never uses a forbidden slope, so the obtained configurations are\nvalid.\n\nConsequently **all** integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) are attainable, and no\nother integer can occur because \\(k\\) is by definition a non\u2011negative integer\nnot exceeding the total number of lines \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal),\n\\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines,\nexactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that together contain all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nPut  \n\n\\[\nt=n-k\\qquad (0\\le t\\le n).\n\\]\n\n**(i) Non\u2011sunny part.**  \nConsider the \\(t\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{\\,y=j\\mid j=1,2,\\dots ,t\\,\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nEach line of \\(\\mathcal H\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(b\\le t\\) lies on exactly one line of\n\\(\\mathcal H\\).\n\n**(ii) The uncovered set.**  \nThe points of \\(S_n\\) not covered by \\(\\mathcal H\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid b>t\\}\n    =\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le k,\\;t+1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(T_k\\) is a right\u2011angled triangle of side length \\(k\\); it\ncontains \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) lattice points.\n\n**(iii) Sunny covering of the triangle.**  \n\n*First sunny line.*  \nThe points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,t+i)\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,k)\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nsatisfy \\(x-y=t\\); therefore they lie on the line  \n\n\\[\nL_0:\\;x-y=t .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_0\\) is \\(1\\); consequently \\(L_0\\) is sunny.  It covers the\n\\(k\\) points listed in (7).\n\n*Remaining points.*  \nAfter removing the points of (7) from \\(T_k\\) we are left with  \n\n\\[\nQ_i=(i,t+1)\\qquad(i=2,3,\\dots ,k),\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\ni.e. exactly \\(k-1\\) points.  \nFor each \\(i\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y-(t+1)=2\\bigl(x-i\\bigr)\\qquad(i=2,\\dots ,k).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of every \\(L_i\\) equals \\(2\\), which is different from\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \nBecause the intercepts \\(-2i-(t+1)\\) are distinct, the lines\n\\(L_2,\\dots ,L_k\\) are pairwise distinct and none of them coincides with\n\\(L_0\\).\n\nThus the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S=\\{L_0\\}\\cup\\{L_i\\mid i=2,\\dots ,k\\}\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nconsists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and together with \\(\\mathcal H\\) covers\nall points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal S\\) are distinct, because the slopes of\nthe lines in \\(\\mathcal H\\) are \\(0\\) while those in \\(\\mathcal S\\) are\n\\(1\\) or \\(2\\).\n\nHence we have produced \\(n=t+k\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are\nsunny, covering the whole set \\(S_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nIf a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition,\nthen the number of sunny lines is necessarily an integer between \\(0\\) and\n\\(n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy definition a line is either sunny or not sunny, and there are exactly \\(n\\)\nlines.  Therefore the number of sunny lines is a non\u2011negative integer not\nexceeding \\(n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem  \nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of all possible numbers \\(k\\) of sunny\nlines in a configuration satisfying the two conditions of the problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLemma\u202f2 shows that only integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can occur.\nLemma\u202f1 shows that for every such integer a configuration with exactly that\nmany sunny lines exists.  Consequently the whole interval \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\)\nis attainable and no other value is possible. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely the\nintegers from \\(0\\) to \\(n\\).  Hence the answer to the problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
