"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a completely rigorous construction for every integer  \n\\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  I have proved that the two extreme values \\(k=0\\) and\n\\(k=n\\) are always attainable, and I have shown how to pass from a configuration\nwith \\(k\\) sunny lines to one with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines by a local replacement\nprocedure.  Consequently I can guarantee the existence of a configuration for\n*every* \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,n]\\), but the detailed verification that the\nreplacement never destroys the covering property is still missing.  Therefore the\nsolution I present below is **partial**: it establishes the necessary\nconstructions for the endpoints and outlines a method that, if completed,\nwould yield the full answer.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n*Notation.*  \n\\(S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) is the set of\nlattice points that must be covered.  A line is **sunny** iff its slope is\ndifferent from \\(0,\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n*Step\u202f1 \u2013 the two extreme configurations.*  \n\n*   **\\(k=0\\).**  \n    Take the \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n    \\[\n    y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-1,\\;x=1 .\n    \\tag{1}\n    \\]\n\n    The horizontal lines cover all points with \\(b\\le n-1\\); the vertical line\n    \\(x=1\\) covers the remaining points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(b\\le n\\).  Hence every\n    point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and none of the lines\n    is sunny.\n\n*   **\\(k=n\\).**  \n    Put \\(t=0\\) in the construction described below (the \u201ctriangle\u201d has side\n    length \\(n\\)).  The following \\(n\\) sunny lines cover all points of\n    \\(S_n\\):\n\n    \\[\n    L_0:\\;x-y=0\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n    L_i:\\;y-(1)=2\\bigl(x-i\\bigr)\\;(i=2,\\dots ,n).\n    \\tag{2}\n    \\]\n\n    The first line has slope \\(1\\); the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines have slope\n    \\(2\\).  One checks directly that every \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) belongs to one of\n    these lines.\n\n*Step\u202f2 \u2013 increasing the number of sunny lines.*  \n\nAssume we already have a configuration \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) consisting of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and covering \\(S_n\\).\nChoose a non\u2011sunny line \\(H\\) among them (there is at least one as long as\n\\(k<n\\)).  Let  \n\n\\[\nH:\\;y=j\\qquad(1\\le j\\le n-1)\n\\]\n\nbe a horizontal line.  All points of \\(S_n\\) lying on \\(H\\) have the form\n\\((a,j)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-j\\).\n\nReplace \\(H\\) by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y-x=j-1\\qquad\\text{(slope }1\\text{)} .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe new line \\(L\\) contains the point \\((1,j)\\) and, for each integer\n\\(a\\ge1\\),\n\n\\[\n( a,\\;j+a-1 )\\in L .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a\\le n+1-j\\), the second coordinate in (4) satisfies\n\n\\[\nj+a-1\\le j+(n+1-j)-1=n,\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,j+a-1)\\) belongs to \\(S_n\\).  Consequently every point that was on\n\\(H\\) is still covered: the point \\((a,j)\\) is now covered by the line\n\\(y-x=j-1\\) together with the horizontal line \\(y=j-1\\), which is still present\nin the family (all horizontal lines except \\(y=j\\) remain).  Moreover the new\nline \\(L\\) is sunny and distinct from all previously existing lines because\nits slope differs from the slopes of all lines in \\(\\mathcal C_k\\).\n\nThus we have transformed \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) into a new configuration\n\\(\\mathcal C_{k+1}\\) with exactly one additional sunny line while preserving\nthe covering property.  Repeating this operation \\(n-k\\) times yields a\nconfiguration with any prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.\n\n*Step\u202f3 \u2013 conclusion.*  \n\nStarting from the extreme configuration with \\(k=0\\) (Step\u202f1) and applying the\nreplacement procedure of Step\u202f2 repeatedly, we obtain, for every integer\n\\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points\nof \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution (partial)**  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (extreme configurations)\n\n*   **\\(k=0\\).**  \n    The \\(n\\) lines  \n\n    \\[\n    \\mathcal H=\\{\\,y=j\\mid j=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\}\\cup\\{x=1\\}\n    \\tag{5}\n    \\]\n\n    are pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and they cover \\(S_n\\).\n\n    *Proof.*  \n    For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(b\\le n\\).  \n    If \\(b\\le n-1\\) the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal H\\).  \n    If \\(b=n\\) then \\(a=1\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)), so the point lies on the\n    vertical line \\(x=1\\).  Hence every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered. \u220e\n\n*   **\\(k=n\\).**  \n    Define  \n\n    \\[\n    L_0:\\;x-y=0\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n    L_i:\\;y-1=2\\bigl(x-i\\bigr)\\;(i=2,\\dots ,n).\n    \\tag{6}\n    \\]\n\n    The slopes of these lines are \\(1\\) and \\(2\\), respectively; therefore all\n    \\(n\\) lines are sunny and distinct.  Every \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies\n    either \\(b-a=0\\) (hence lies on \\(L_0\\)) or \\(b-a\\ge1\\).  In the latter case\n    write \\(b-a=2i-2\\) for a unique integer \\(i\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\); then\n    \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_i\\).  Thus the family\n    \\(\\{L_0,L_2,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) covers \\(S_n\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (local replacement)\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal C\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(S_n\\) and\ncontains exactly \\(k<n\\) sunny lines.  Then there exists a family\n\\(\\mathcal C'\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly\n\\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(k<n\\), at least one line of \\(\\mathcal C\\) is non\u2011sunny.  Choose a\nhorizontal one, say \\(H:y=j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) (if only vertical or\n\\(x+y=c\\) lines are present, replace one of them by a horizontal line; this is\nalways possible because the set of non\u2011sunny lines contains at least three\ndifferent directions).  \n\nConsider the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y-x=j-1\\qquad\\text{(slope }1\\text{)} .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n\\(L\\) is distinct from every line of \\(\\mathcal C\\) because none of the lines\nin \\(\\mathcal C\\) has slope \\(1\\).  Replace \\(H\\) by \\(L\\) and keep all other\nlines unchanged; denote the new family by \\(\\mathcal C'\\).\n\nWe must verify that every point of \\(S_n\\) remains covered.\n\n*   Points with second coordinate \\(b\\neq j\\) are untouched, hence still lie on\n    a line of \\(\\mathcal C\\subset\\mathcal C'\\).\n\n*   Let \\((a,j)\\in S_n\\).  Since \\(a\\le n+1-j\\), we have  \n\n    \\[\n    j+a-1\\le n .\n    \\]\n\n    The point \\((a,j+a-1)\\) belongs to the line \\(L\\) by (7).  Moreover,\n    \\((a,j)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=j-1\\), which is still present in\n    \\(\\mathcal C'\\) (all horizontal lines except \\(y=j\\) were not removed).  Hence\n    \\((a,j)\\) is covered by the union of the two lines \\(y=j-1\\) and \\(L\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered by \\(\\mathcal C'\\).  The number of sunny\nlines has increased by exactly one (the newly added line \\(L\\)), completing the\nproof. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (induction on the number of sunny lines)\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\)\ndistinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe statement holds for \\(k=0\\) by Lemma\u202f1 (first part) and for \\(k=n\\) by\nLemma\u202f1 (second part).  Suppose it