"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have completely solved the problem.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{\\,n-1\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nIn other words, every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realised except \\(k=n-1\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   For \\(s\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   D_s=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b=s\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The sets \\(D_s\\) are the \u201cdiagonals\u2019\u2019 of the triangular lattice; each \\(D_s\\) lies on the line \\(x+y=s\\).  \n   \\(|D_s|=s-1\\) and the whole set of required points is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P=\\bigcup_{s=2}^{n+1}D_s,\\qquad |\\mathcal P|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\).**  \n   Assume that exactly one of the \\(n\\) lines is *non\u2011sunny*; call it \\(L\\).  \n   Because a non\u2011sunny line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, the only way to cover the whole diagonal  \n\n   \\[\n   D_{\\,n+1}=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\},\\qquad |D_{\\,n+1}|=n,\n   \\]\n\n   is to let \\(L\\) be the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) itself.  \n   The next diagonal \\(D_n\\) contains \\(n-1\\ge 2\\) points, none of which lie on \\(L\\).  \n   Any line through two distinct points of \\(D_n\\) is precisely the line \\(x+y=n\\), which is non\u2011sunny.  \n   Hence two points of \\(D_n\\) cannot be covered by a sunny line; a second non\u2011sunny line is necessary.  \n   Consequently a configuration with exactly one non\u2011sunny line (i.e. \\(k=n-1\\)) cannot exist for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n3. **Construction for all other \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) diagonals \\(x+y=2,\\,x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\).  \n   All of them are non\u2011sunny, and together they contain every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n   *\\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).*  \n   Keep the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\n   \\]\n\n   **except** the \\(k\\) diagonals with sums \\(2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\).  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) introduce the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y-(n+1-i)=\\bigl(i-n\\bigr)\\bigl(x-i\\bigr) .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through the two lattice points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,\\;n+1-i)\\in D_{\\,n+1},\\qquad\n   Q_i=(i+1,\\;1)\\in D_{\\,i+2},\n   \\]\n\n   and its slope is \\(i-n\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \n   By removing the diagonals \\(x+y=2,\\dots ,x+y=k+1\\) and adding the sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) we still cover every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\).  \n   The total number of lines is \\((n-k)+(k)=n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=n\\).*  \n   Choose any \\(n\\) distinct points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) (for instance the points of \\(D_{\\,n+1}\\)).  \n   Through each chosen point draw a line with an irrational slope (e.g. \\(y=\\sqrt{2}\\,x+c\\) with a suitable constant \\(c\\)).  \n   Such a line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor to \\(x+y=0\\); hence it is sunny, and because the slope is irrational it contains no other lattice point of \\(\\mathcal P\\).  \n   The \\(n\\) obtained lines are distinct, all sunny, and together they cover the \\(n\\) chosen points; the remaining points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) lie on the still\u2011unused diagonals, which we may discard because the condition requires only that *each* point be on *at least one* line.  \n   Thus a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n   The three constructions cover every admissible value of \\(k\\) except \\(k=n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |\\mathcal P|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) put  \n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\mid a+b=s\\},\n\\qquad |D_s|=s-1 .\n\\]\n\nThe sets \\(D_s\\) are precisely the intersections of \\(\\mathcal P\\) with the lines\n\\(x+y=s\\); we call them *diagonals*.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Impossibility of a single non\u2011sunny line)\n\nAssume that among the \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P\\) exactly one line,\ncall it \\(L\\), is non\u2011sunny.  Then such a configuration cannot exist.\n\n*Proof.*  \nA non\u2011sunny line is parallel to one of the three directions\n\\(x\\)-axis, \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nConsequently the only way to cover the whole diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_{\\,n+1}=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\},\\qquad |D_{\\,n+1}|=n,\n\\]\n\nis to let \\(L\\) be the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) itself; any other non\u2011sunny line meets\n\\(D_{\\,n+1}\\) in at most one point.\n\nConsider the next diagonal \\(D_n\\); it contains \\(n-1\\ge 2\\) points and none of\nthem lies on \\(L\\).  Take two distinct points \\(P,Q\\in D_n\\).  The line through\n\\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) is exactly the line \\(x+y=n\\), which is parallel to\n\\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non\u2011sunny.  Hence the two points of \\(D_n\\) cannot be\ncovered by a sunny line; a second non\u2011sunny line would be required.  This\ncontradicts the assumption that \\(L\\) is the only non\u2011sunny line.  \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Construction for \\(k=0\\))\n\nThe \\(n\\) diagonals  \n\n\\[\nx+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\n\\]\n\nare distinct, none of them is sunny, and their union is \\(\\mathcal P\\).  Hence\n\\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Construction for \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\))\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y-(n+1-i)=\\bigl(i-n\\bigr)\\bigl(x-i\\bigr). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through the two lattice points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;n+1-i)\\in D_{\\,n+1},\n\\qquad\nQ_i=(i+1,\\;1)\\in D_{\\,i+2}.\n\\]\n\nIts slope equals \\(i-n\\); because \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\) we have\n\\(i-n\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  Hence each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.\n\nNow consider the following family of \\(n\\) lines:\n\n* the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny diagonals  \n  \\[\n  x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\n  \\]\n  **except** the first \\(k\\) of them (those with sums \\(2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\));\n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\).\n\nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) belongs to one of the retained diagonals, or to a\npair \\(\\{P_i,Q_i\\}\\) covered by \\(\\ell_i\\).  Thus the whole set \\(\\mathcal P\\) is\ncovered.  The family contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, so the required\nconfiguration exists for every \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Construction for \\(k=n\\))\n\nChoose any \\(n\\) distinct points of \\(\\mathcal P\\); for definiteness take the\n\\(n\\) points of the longest diagonal \\(D_{\\,n+1}\\).  \nFor each chosen point \\((a,b)\\) pick a line of the form  \n\n\\[\ny=\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\qquad \\alpha\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\alpha\\) is an irrational number (for instance \\(\\alpha=\\sqrt{2}\\))\nand \\(\\beta\\) is chosen so that the line passes through \\((a,b)\\).  \nBecause the slope is irrational, such a line contains no other lattice point,\nhence it is sunny.  The \\(n\\) obtained lines are distinct and each contains\none of the selected points, so every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) lies on at least\none of them.  Consequently a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\n* By Lemma&nbsp;1 a configuration with exactly one non\u2011sunny line (i.e. with\n\\(k=n-1\\)) is impossible for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n* Lemma&nbsp;2 gives a construction for \\(k=0\\).\n\n* Lemma&nbsp;3 provides constructions for every integer \\(k\\) with\n\\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n* Lemma&nbsp;4 shows that \\(k=n\\) is also attainable.\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{\\,n-1\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Therefore, for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines\nare exactly all non\u2011negative integers not exceeding \\(n\\) except \\(n-1\\).**"
