"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have completely solved the problem.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{\\,n-1\\,\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. every integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) can be realised except \\(k=n-1\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n*Step\u202f1 \u2013 Notation.*  \nFor \\(s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\) let  \n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b=s\\},\n\\qquad |D_s|=s-1 .\n\\]\n\nThe required set of points is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\bigcup_{s=2}^{n+1}D_s .\n\\]\n\n*Step\u202f2 \u2013 Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\).*  \nAssume exactly one of the \\(n\\) lines is non\u2011sunny.  \nA non\u2011sunny line can be parallel only to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line\n\\(x+y=0\\).  \nTo cover the whole diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) (which contains \\(n\\) points) the\nnon\u2011sunny line must be the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) itself.  \nThe next diagonal \\(D_n\\) contains \\(n-1\\ge2\\) points, none of which lie on that\nline.  Any line through two points of \\(D_n\\) has slope \\(-1\\) and is therefore\nnon\u2011sunny.  Consequently at least two non\u2011sunny lines are needed, contradicting\nthe assumption.  Hence a configuration with exactly one non\u2011sunny line\n(\\(k=n-1\\)) does **not** exist.\n\n*Step\u202f3 \u2013 Constructions for all other values of \\(k\\).*\n\n*   **\\(k=0\\).**  \n    Take the \\(n\\) diagonals \\(x+y=2,\\,x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\).  \n    All are non\u2011sunny and together they contain \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*   **\\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n    Keep the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny diagonals  \n\n    \\[\n    x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\n    \\]\n\n    **except** the first \\(k\\) of them (those with sums \\(2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\)).  \n    For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) introduce the sunny line  \n\n    \\[\n    \\ell_i:\\;y-(n+1-i)=(i-n)\\bigl(x-i\\bigr) .\n    \\tag{1}\n    \\]\n\n    The line \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through the two lattice points  \n\n    \\[\n    P_i=(i,\\;n+1-i)\\in D_{\\,n+1},\n    \\qquad\n    Q_i=(i+1,\\;1)\\in D_{\\,i+2},\n    \\]\n\n    and its slope \\(i-n\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore\n    \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \n    By deleting the diagonals \\(x+y=2,\\dots ,x+y=k+1\\) and adding the sunny\n    lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) we still cover every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\);\n    the total number of lines is \\(n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n*   **\\(k=n\\).**  \n    For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) define a line \\(L_i\\) through the two points  \n\n    \\[\n    P_i=(i,\\;n+1-i)\\in D_{\\,n+1},\n    \\qquad\n    Q_i=\n    \\begin{cases}\n    (i+1,\\;1), & 1\\le i\\le n-2,\\\\[2mm]\n    (1,\\;i+2-n), & i=n-1,\\\\[2mm]\n    (1,\\;2), & i=n .\n    \\end{cases}\n    \\tag{2}\n    \\]\n\n    *All points \\(Q_i\\) belong to \\(\\mathcal P\\):*  \n    for \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\) we have \\(Q_i\\in D_{\\,i+2}\\);  \n    for \\(i=n-1\\) we have \\(Q_{n-1}=(1,1)\\in D_{2}\\);  \n    for \\(i=n\\) we have \\(Q_{n}=(1,2)\\in D_{3}\\).  \n    Hence the set \\(\\{Q_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\) equals the union of the diagonals\n    \\(D_{2},D_{3},\\dots ,D_{n}\\).\n\n    *The slopes are never forbidden.*  \n    For \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\) the slope of \\(L_i\\) equals  \n\n    \\[\n    \\frac{1-(n+1-i)}{(i+1)-i}=i-n\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n    \\]\n\n    For \\(i=n-1\\) the slope is  \n\n    \\[\n    \\frac{1-(1)}{1-(n-1)}=0,\n    \\]\n\n    but the two points are \\((n-1,2)\\) and \\((1,1)\\); the actual computation gives  \n\n    \\[\n    \\frac{1-2}{1-(n-1)}=\\frac{-1}{2-n}= \\frac{1}{n-2},\n    \\]\n\n    which is again different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n    For \\(i=n\\) the slope is  \n\n    \\[\n    \\frac{2- (n+1-n)}{1-n}= \\frac{1}{1-n},\n    \\]\n\n    also admissible.  Consequently every line \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\n    *Coverage.*  \n    Each point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) appears either as some \\(P_i\\) (all points of\n    \\(D_{n+1}\\)) or as some \\(Q_i\\) (all points of the remaining diagonals\n    \\(D_{2},\\dots ,D_{n}\\)).  Hence the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\)\n    together cover the whole set \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\nThus we have explicit constructions for every admissible value of \\(k\\) and have proved that \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\mid a+b=s\\}\\;(2\\le s\\le n+1).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(D_s\\) lies on the line \\(x+y=s\\) and \\(|D_s|=s-1\\); moreover  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\bigcup_{s=2}^{n+1}D_s,\\qquad \n\\bigl| \\mathcal P \\bigr|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (No configuration with exactly one non\u2011sunny line)\n\nAssume that among the \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P\\) exactly one line,\ncall it \\(L\\), is non\u2011sunny.  A non\u2011sunny line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis,\nthe \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nTo contain the whole diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) (which has \\(n\\) points) the line \\(L\\)\nmust be the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) itself; any other non\u2011sunny line meets \\(D_{n+1}\\) in\nat most one point.\n\nThe next diagonal \\(D_n\\) contains \\(n-1\\ge2\\) points, none of which lie on\n\\(L\\).  Any line through two distinct points of \\(D_n\\) has slope \\(-1\\) and is\ntherefore non\u2011sunny.  Hence the points of \\(D_n\\) cannot be covered by a sunny\nline; a second non\u2011sunny line would be required.  This contradicts the\nassumption that \\(L\\) is the only non\u2011sunny line.  \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Existence for \\(k=0\\))\n\nThe \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nx+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\n\\]\n\nare distinct, none of them is sunny, and their union equals \\(\\mathcal P\\).\nThus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Existence for \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\))\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;y-(n+1-i)=(i-n)\\bigl(x-i\\bigr). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;n+1-i)\\in D_{\\,n+1},\n\\qquad\nQ_i=(i+1,\\;1)\\in D_{\\,i+2},\n\\]\n\nand its slope equals \\(i-n\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore\n\\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.\n\nConsider the family consisting of  \n\n* the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny diagonals  \n\n  \\[\n  x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\n  \\]\n\n  **except** the first \\(k\\) of them (those with sums \\(2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\));\n\n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\).\n\nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) belongs either to one of the retained diagonals\nor to a pair \\(\\{P_i,Q_i\\}\\); consequently the whole set \\(\\mathcal P\\) is\ncovered.  The family contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely\n\\(k\\) are sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Existence for \\(k=n\\))\n\nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) define a line \\(L_i\\) through the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;n+1-i)\\in D_{\\,n+1},\n\\qquad\nQ_i=\n\\begin{cases}\n(i+1,\\;1), & 1\\le i\\le n-2,\\\\[2mm]\n(1,\\;i+2-n), & i=n-1,\\\\[2mm]\n(1,\\;2), & i=n .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAll points \\(Q_i\\) lie in \\(\\mathcal P\\):\nfor \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\) we have \\(Q_i\\in D_{\\,i+2}\\);\n\\(Q_{n-1}=(1,1)\\in D_{2}\\);\n\\(Q_{n}=(1,2)\\in D_{3}\\).\nHence the set \\(\\{Q_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\) equals the union of the diagonals\n\\(D_{2},D_{3},\\dots ,D_{n}\\).\n\nThe slopes of the lines are\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{slope}(L_i)=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\displaystyle\\frac{1-(n+1-i)}{(i+1)-i}=i-n, & 1\\le i\\le n-2,\\\\[3mm]\n\\displaystyle\\frac{(i+2-n)-2}{1-(n-1)}=\\frac{1}{\\,n-2\\,}, & i=n-1,\\\\[3mm]\n\\displaystyle\\frac{2- (n+1-n)}{1-n}= \\frac{1}{\\,1-n\\,}, & i=n .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nEach of these numbers is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore every line\n\\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) appears either as some \\(P_i\\) (all points of the\nlongest diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\)) or as some \\(Q_i\\) (all points of the remaining\ndiagonals).  Consequently the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) together\ncover the whole set \\(\\mathcal P\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\n* Lemma\u202f1 shows that \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible.\n* Lemma\u202f2 provides a configuration for \\(k=0\\).\n* Lemma\u202f3 gives configurations for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n* Lemma\u202f4 supplies a configuration for \\(k=n\\).\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{\\,n-1\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Therefore, for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines are\nall non\u2011negative integers not exceeding \\(n\\) except \\(n-1\\).**"
