nny line.  \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Existence for \\(k=0\\))\n\nThe \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nx+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\n\\]\n\nare distinct, none of them is sunny, and their union equals \\(\\mathcal P\\).\nThus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Existence for \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\))\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;y-(n+1-i)=(i-n)\\bigl(x-i\\bigr). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;n+1-i)\\in D_{\\,n+1},\n\\qquad\nQ_i=(i+1,\\;1)\\in D_{\\,i+2},\n\\]\n\nand its slope equals \\(i-n\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore\n\\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.\n\nConsider the family consisting of  \n\n* the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny diagonals  \n\n  \\[\n  x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\n  \\]\n\n  **except** the first \\(k\\) of them (those with sums \\(2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\));\n\n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\).\n\nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) belongs either to one of the retained diagonals\nor to a pair \\(\\{P_i,Q_i\\}\\); consequently the whole set \\(\\mathcal P\\) is\ncovered.  The family contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely\n\\(k\\) are sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Existence for \\(k=n\\))\n\nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) define a line \\(L_i\\) through the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;n+1-i)\\in D_{\\,n+1},\n\\qquad\nQ_i=\n\\begin{cases}\n(i+1,\\;1), & 1\\le i\\le n-2,\\\\[2mm]\n(1,\\;i+2-n), & i=n-1,\\\\[2mm]\n(1,\\;2), & i=n .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAll points \\(Q_i\\) lie in \\(\\mathcal P\\):\nfor \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\) we have \\(Q_i\\in D_{\\,i+2}\\);\n\\(Q_{n-1}=(1,1)\\in D_{2}\\);\n\\(Q_{n}=(1,2)\\in D_{3}\\).\nHence the set \\(\\{Q_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\) equals the union of the diagonals\n\\(D_{2},D_{3},\\dots ,D_{n}\\).\n\nThe slopes of the lines are\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{slope}(L_i)=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\displaystyle\\frac{1-(n+1-i)}{(i+1)-i}=i-n, & 1\\le i\\le n-2,\\\\[3mm]\n\\displaystyle\\frac{(i+2-n)-2}{1-(n-1)}=\\frac{1}{\\,n-2\\,}, & i=n-1,\\\\[3mm]\n\\displaystyle\\frac{2- (n+1-n)}{1-n}= \\frac{1}{\\,1-n\\,}, & i=n .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nEach of these numbers is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore every line\n\\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) appears either as some \\(P_i\\) (all points of the\nlongest diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\)) or as some \\(Q_i\\) (all points of the remaining\ndiagonals).  Consequently the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) together\ncover the whole set \\(\\mathcal P\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\n* Lemma\u202f1 shows that \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible.\n* Lemma\u202f2 provides a configuration for \\(k=0\\).\n* Lemma\u202f3 gives configurations for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n* Lemma\u202f4 supplies a configuration for \\(k=n\\).\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{\\,n-1\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Therefore, for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines are\nall non\u2011negative integers not exceeding \\(n\\) except \\(n-1\\).**\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
