nductive increase of the number of sunny lines.*  \n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal C\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and\ncontaining exactly \\(t<n\\) sunny lines.  We shall construct a new family\n\\(\\mathcal C'\\) with the same size, still covering \\(S_n\\), and with\n\\(\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal C')=t+1\\).\n\n1. **Choice of a replaceable non\u2011sunny line.**  \n   Because \\(t<n\\), at least one line of \\(\\mathcal C\\) is non\u2011sunny.  Among the\n   non\u2011sunny lines pick a horizontal one; denote it by  \n\n   \\[\n   H:\\;y=j\\qquad (1\\le j\\le n-1).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   (If \\(\\mathcal C\\) contains no horizontal non\u2011sunny line, then it must contain\n   the vertical line \\(x=1\\); in that case we first replace \\(x=1\\) by the sunny\n   line \\(y-x=n-1\\) \u2013 this is a special case of the construction below with\n   \\(j=n\\).)\n\n2. **Selection of a new slope.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal S=\\{\\text{slope of each line in }\\mathcal C\\}.\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The set \\(\\mathcal S\\) is finite, so we can choose a rational number\n   \\(\\alpha\\notin\\mathcal S\\) with \\(\\alpha\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  (For instance,\n   take \\(\\alpha=2\\) unless \\(2\\in\\mathcal S\\); otherwise take \\(\\alpha=3\\), etc.)\n   The line with slope \\(\\alpha\\) that passes through the point \\((1,j)\\) is\n\n   \\[\n   L:\\;y-j=\\alpha\\,(x-1)\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad\n   y=\\alpha x+(j-\\alpha).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   By construction \\(L\\) is sunny and its slope does not coincide with the slope\n   of any line already present in \\(\\mathcal C\\); consequently \\(L\\) is distinct\n   from every line of \\(\\mathcal C\\).\n\n3. **The replacement.**  \n   Form  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal C'=(\\mathcal C\\setminus\\{H\\})\\cup\\{L\\}.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   The family \\(\\mathcal C'\\) still consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, and\n   \\(\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal C')=t+1\\) because e