uation\u202f(12))\n*Claim:*  \n\n\\[\nL_0:\\;x-y=0,\\qquad\nL_i:\\;y-1=2\\bigl(x-i\\bigr)\\;(i=2,3,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\nare \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Verification:*  \n- Slopes: \\(L_0\\) has slope \\(1\\); each \\(L_i\\) has slope \\(2\\). Both are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so all lines are sunny.  \n- The proof asserts that for any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) the difference \\(b-a\\) belongs to \\(\\{0,2,4,\\dots ,2n-2\\}\\). This is **false**.  \n  Counterexample: take \\(n=5\\) and the point \\((1,2)\\in S_5\\). Here \\(b-a=1\\), which is odd and not in the claimed set. Direct substitution shows that \\((1,2)\\) satisfies none of the equations \\(x-y=0\\) or \\(y-1=2(x-i)\\) for any integer \\(i\\). Hence \\((1,2)\\) is not covered.  \n- Therefore the family does **not** cover all of \\(S_n\\). The argument that \u201c\\(b-a\\) is always even\u201d is incorrect; the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) imposes no parity restriction on \\(b-a\\).\n\n*Conclusion:* **Critical Error** \u2013 Lemma\u202f1, part\u202f2 is false, so the claimed existence of a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines is not established.\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f2 (inductive replacement)\n\n*Statement:* Starting from a configuration \\(\\mathcal C\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) with \\(t<n\\) sunny lines, one can replace a non\u2011sunny line by a sunny line to obtain a new configuration \\(\\mathcal C'\\) with \\(t+1\\) sunny lines, still covering \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof Sketch in Solution:*  \n1. Choose a horizontal non\u2011sunny line \\(H: y=j\\) (or the vertical line \\(x=1\\) handled separately).  \n2. Let \\(\\mathcal S\\) be the set of slopes already present; pick a rational \\(\\alpha\\) not in \\(\\mathcal S\\) and not equal to \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n3. Define the new sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L:\\; y-j = \\alpha (x-1) \\quad\\text{(equation\u202f(16))}.\n   \\]\n\n4. Set \\(\\mathcal C' = (\\mathcal C\\setminus\\{H\\})\\cup\\{L\\}\\).  \n5. Argu