sfies  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal S_n}\\ell\\supseteq S_n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAll lines \\(L_i\\) have slope \\(2\\), which is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\)\nand \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  The intercept of \\(L_i\\) is\n\\(1-2i\\); distinct values of \\(i\\) give distinct intercepts, so the lines\nare pairwise different.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(a\\le n\\).  Set\n\\(i=n+1-a\\); then \\(1\\le i\\le n\\).  Substituting \\(i\\) into the equation of\n\\(L_i\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ny=2x+1-2(n+1-a)=2x-2n-1+2a .\n\\]\n\nEvaluating at \\(x=a\\) yields  \n\n\\[\ny=2a-2n-1+2a=4a-2n-1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad 4a-2n-1\\ge b .\n\\]\n\nBut the left\u2013hand side equals the right\u2013hand side exactly when\n\\(b=2a+(1-2i)\\), i.e. when \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_i\\).  Because the\ninequality above is an equality for the chosen \\(i\\), the point\n\\((a,b)\\) indeed satisfies the equation of \\(L_i\\); hence \\((a,b)\\in L_i\\).\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one line of\n\\(\\mathcal S_n\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines\nin a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of\n\\(S_n\\) contains at least the two values \\(0\\) and \\(n\\).  The inductive\nprocedure that would produce the intermediate values \\(k\\) with\n\\(0<k<n\\) is not valid, because after removing a non\u2011sunny line there is\nno finite way to keep all points covered while preserving the total\nnumber of lines.  Consequently the full description of the admissible\nset of \\(k\\) remains open.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In