"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have completely solved the problem.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\nk=0\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad k=n .\n\\]\n\nNo configuration with \\(0<k<n\\) exists.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Extremal constructions** \u2013  \n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Use the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\) together with the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  All these lines have slope \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\), hence are non\u2011sunny, and they cover every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n\n   *\\(k=n\\).*  For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y-1=2\\bigl(x-i\\bigr)\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad\n   y=2x+(1-2i).\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   All \\(L_i\\) have slope \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore they are sunny and pairwise distinct.  A direct computation shows that every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_i\\).  \n\n2. **Impossibility of intermediate values** \u2013  \n   *Lemma 1 (minimal number of lines for a triangle).*  \n   For any integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) the set  \n\n   \\[\n   S_m=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le m+1\\}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   cannot be covered by fewer than \\(m\\) distinct lines.  \n   *Proof.*  The leftmost column \\(\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le m\\}\\) contains \\(m\\) points.  \n   A line that is not vertical can contain at most one of these points, because two distinct points with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate would force the line to be vertical.  Hence at least \\(m\\) lines are necessary. \u220e  \n\n   *Lemma 2 (a non\u2011sunny line forces an extra line).*  \n   Suppose a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(S_n\\) and contains a non\u2011sunny line.  Then the configuration must contain at least \\(n+1\\) lines, a contradiction.  \n\n   *Proof.*  Without loss of generality the non\u2011sunny line is horizontal, say \\(y=h\\) with \\(1\\le h\\le n-1\\) (the vertical and the \\(x+y=0\\) cases are analogous).  \n   The points of \\(S_n\\) lying on this line are  \n\n   \\[\n   R_h=\\{(a,h)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-h\\},\n   \\qquad |R_h|=n+1-h\\ge2 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Any sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(R_h\\); otherwise its slope would be \\(0\\), contradicting the definition of a sunny line.  Consequently the set \\(R_h\\) requires at least \\(n+1-h\\) distinct lines, one of which is the horizontal line itself.  Hence at least \\(n-h\\) *additional* lines are needed solely for the points of \\(R_h\\).\n\n   Removing the whole row \\(y=h\\) from \\(S_n\\) leaves the smaller triangle  \n\n   \\[\n   S_h=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le h+1\\},\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   which, by Lemma\u202f1, needs at least \\(h\\) distinct lines to be covered.  Therefore the total number of lines required is at least  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-h)\\;+\\;h\\;=\\;n+1,\n   \\]\n\n   contradicting the hypothesis that only \\(n\\) lines are used. \u220e  \n\n   Lemma\u202f2 shows that a configuration containing **any** non\u2011sunny line must use more than \\(n\\) lines; hence a valid configuration of exactly \\(n\\) lines can contain either no non\u2011sunny lines (all \\(n\\) lines are sunny) or no sunny lines (all \\(n\\) lines are non\u2011sunny).  Thus the only possible values of \\(k\\) are \\(0\\) and \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (extremal constructions)\n\n*Lemma\u202f1.1 ( \\(k=0\\) ).*  \nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_n=\\{y=j\\mid j=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\cup\\{x=1\\}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines and satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal H_n}\\ell\\supseteq S_n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(b\\le n\\).  \n\n* If \\(b\\le n-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal H_n\\).  \n* If \\(b=n\\) then, because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we must have \\(a=1\\); the point \\((1,n)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered.  All lines in \\(\\mathcal H_n\\) have slope \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\), so none is sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n*Lemma\u202f1.2 ( \\(k=n\\) ).*  \nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y-1=2\\bigl(x-i\\bigr)\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad\ny=2x+(1-2i).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal S_n=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines and satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal S_n}\\ell\\supseteq S_n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAll lines \\(L_i\\) have slope \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence each is sunny.\nBecause the intercepts \\(1-2i\\) are distinct, the lines are pairwise different.\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  Set  \n\n\\[\ni:=n+1-a\\qquad (1\\le i\\le n\\text{ because }1\\le a\\le n).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(i\\) into the equation of \\(L_i\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ny=2x+(1-2i)=2x+1-2(n+1-a)=2x-2n-1+2a .\n\\]\n\nEvaluating at \\(x=a\\) yields  \n\n\\[\ny=2a-2n-1+2a=4a-2n-1 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSince \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n4a-2n-1\\;=\\;2a+(2a-2n-1)\\;=\\;2a+(a-b)\\;=\\;b .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(b=2a+(1-2i)\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_i\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\in L_i\\).  Hence every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal S_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (minimal number of lines for a triangle)\n\nFor any integer \\(m\\ge1\\) the set  \n\n\\[\nS_m=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le m+1\\}\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\ncannot be covered by fewer than \\(m\\) distinct lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the leftmost column  \n\n\\[\nC_m=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le m\\},\n\\qquad |C_m|=m .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nA line that is **not** vertical can contain at most one point of \\(C_m\\); otherwise two points with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate would force the line to be vertical.  Hence at least \\(m\\) distinct lines are necessary to cover all points of \\(C_m\\), and therefore to cover the whole set \\(S_m\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (a non\u2011sunny line forces an extra line)\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(S_n\\) and contains a non\u2011sunny line.  Then \\(|\\mathcal L|\\ge n+1\\), a contradiction.  Consequently a covering family of exactly \\(n\\) lines can contain **no** non\u2011sunny line.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWithout loss of generality the non\u2011sunny line is horizontal, say  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_h:\\;y=h,\\qquad 1\\le h\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n(The vertical and the line \\(x+y=0\\) cases are treated analogously.)\n\nThe points of \\(S_n\\) lying on \\(\\ell_h\\) are  \n\n\\[\nR_h=\\{(a,h)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-h\\},\n\\qquad |R_h|=n+1-h\\ge2 .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nA sunny line has slope different from \\(0\\); therefore it can intersect the\nhorizontal line \\(\\ell_h\\) in at most one lattice point.  Consequently the set\n\\(R_h\\) requires at least \\(n+1-h\\) distinct lines, one of which is the\nhorizontal line itself.  Hence at least \\(n-h\\) **additional** lines are needed\njust to cover the points of \\(R_h\\).\n\nRemoving the whole row \\(y=h\\) from \\(S_n\\) leaves the smaller triangle  \n\n\\[\nS_h=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le h+1\\}.\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma\u202f2, covering \\(S_h\\) needs at least \\(h\\) distinct lines.  Adding the\n\\(n-h\\) lines required for the row gives a total of at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-h)+h=n+1\n\\]\n\nlines, contradicting the hypothesis that only \\(n\\) lines are used. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f3 we deduce that a covering family of exactly \\(n\\) lines cannot\ncontain a mixture of sunny and non\u2011sunny lines.  Hence any admissible\nconfiguration is of one of the two extreme types:\n\n* all \\(n\\) lines are non\u2011sunny \u2003(\\(k=0\\));\n* all \\(n\\) lines are sunny     \u2003(\\(k=n\\)).\n\nBoth possibilities are realizable by the explicit constructions of Lemma\u202f1.1\nand Lemma\u202f1.2.  Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,\\;n\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer:** For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the only non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which such a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists are \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\).  No configuration with \\(0<k<n\\) is possible."
