ines.  Adding the\n\\(n-h\\) lines required for the row gives a total of at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-h)+h=n+1\n\\]\n\nlines, contradicting the hypothesis that only \\(n\\) lines are used. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f3 we deduce that a covering family of exactly \\(n\\) lines cannot\ncontain a mixture of sunny and non\u2011sunny lines.  Hence any admissible\nconfiguration is of one of the two extreme types:\n\n* all \\(n\\) lines are non\u2011sunny \u2003(\\(k=0\\));\n* all \\(n\\) lines are sunny     \u2003(\\(k=n\\)).\n\nBoth possibilities are realizable by the explicit constructions of Lemma\u202f1.1\nand Lemma\u202f1.2.  Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,\\;n\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer:** For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the only non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which such a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists are \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\).  No configuration with \\(0<k<n\\) is possible."
