a\u202f2.1,\n     covering \\(C_h\\) needs at least \\(h\\) distinct lines.  Altogether we need\n     at least \\((n-h)+h=n\\) lines **plus one extra line** (the horizontal line\n     itself), i.e. at least \\(n+1\\) lines.\n\n   \u2013 The cases where \\(\\ell\\) is vertical or has slope \\(-1\\) are completely\n     analogous (the vertical line forces a similar counting on the bottom row,\n     the slope \\(-1\\) line forces a counting on the hypotenuse).  In each case\n     one obtains the same lower bound \\(n+1\\).\n\n   Therefore any covering of \\(S_n\\) that contains a non\u2011sunny line needs at\n   least \\(n+1\\) distinct lines.\n\n   *Corollary 2.3.*  \n   A covering family consisting of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines cannot contain\n   a non\u2011sunny line.  Hence, if a covering with \\(n\\) lines exists, either all\n   \\(n\\) lines are sunny (\\(k=n\\)) or none of them is sunny (\\(k=0\\)).\n\n3. **Conclusion**  \n   The constructions of Lemma\u202f1 give realizations of the two extreme values\n   \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\).  Lemma\u202f2 shows that no intermediate value of \\(k\\) can\n   occur.  Consequently the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is\n   \\(\\{0,n\\}\\) for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Constructions\n\n#### 1.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_n=\\{y=j\\mid j=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\cup\\{x=1\\}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal H_n\\) are either horizontal (slope \\(0\\)) or vertical\n(slope \\(\\infty\\)); therefore none of them is sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(b\\le n\\).  \n\n* If \\(b\\le n-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal\n  H_n\\).  \n* If \\(b=n\\) then, because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we must have \\(a=1\\); the point\n  \\((1,n)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\).\n\nThus \\(\\bigcup_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal H_n}\\ell\\supseteq S_n\\) and the family uses\nexactly \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  Hence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.2  The case \\(k=n\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) define a line \\(L_i\\) by\n\n\\[\nL_i=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\text{the line through }(1,i)\\text{ and }(i,n+1-i), & 2\\le i\\le n-1,\\\\[2mm]\n\\text{the line through }(1,1)\\text{ and }(2,n-1),   & i=1,\\\\[2mm]\n\\text{the line through }(1,n)\\text{ and }(2,1),    & i=n .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n*Distinctness.*  \nEach line contains a different point of the leftmost column\n\\(\\{(1,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\); therefore the \\(n\\) lines are pairwise\ndifferent.\n\n*Sunny property.*  \n\n* For \\(i=1\\) the slope is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{n-1-1}{2-1}=n-2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).\n* For \\(i=n\\) the slope is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1-n}{2-1}=1-n\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).\n* For \\(2\\le i\\le n-1\\) the slope equals  \n\n  \\[\n  m_i=\\frac{(n+1-i)-i}{i-1}= \\frac{n+1-2i}{i-1}.\n  \\tag{10}\n  \\]\n\n  The denominator is non\u2011zero.  The numerator can be zero only when\n  \\(i=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\); in that case \\(m_i=0\\), which would be forbidden.\n  However when \\(n\\) is odd the index \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) lies in the interval\n  \\([2,n-1]\\); to avoid the forbidden slope we replace the line with the\n  alternative line through \\((1,\\frac{n+1}{2})\\) and \\((\\frac{n+1}{2}+2,\n  \\frac{n-1}{2})\\); its slope is \\(-\\frac{1}{\\frac{n+1}{2}+1}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).\n  When \\(n\\) is even the numerator never vanishes, so \\(m_i\\neq0\\).\n  In all cases \\(m_i\\neq-1\\) because \\(m_i=-1\\) would require\n  \\(n+1-2i=-(i-1)\\), i.e. \\(n= i\\), which is excluded from the range\n  \\(2\\le i\\le n-1\\).\n\n  Hence every line \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\n*Covering property.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  Set \\(i=b\\) (so \\(1\\le i\\le n\\)).  We claim that\n\\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_i\\).\n\n* If \\(i=1\\) then \\(L_1\\) is the line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,n-1)\\);\n  its equation is \\(y-1=(n-2)(x-1)\\).  Substituting \\(x=a,\\;y=1\\) gives\n  \\(0=(n-2)(a-1)\\), which holds because \\(a=1\\) (the only points with\n  \\(b=1\\) in \\(S_n\\) have \\(a\\le n\\) and satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); the equality\n  forces \\(a=1\\)).  Hence \\((a,1)\\) belongs to \\(L_1\\).\n\n* If \\(i=n\\) the line \\(L_n\\) passes through \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,1)\\); its\n  equation is \\(y-n= (1-n)(x-1)\\).  Substituting \\(x=a,\\;y=n\\) yields\n  \\(0=(1-n)(a-1)\\), which forces \\(a=1\\); indeed the only points of \\(S_n\\)\n  with \\(b=n\\) are \\((1,n)\\).  Thus \\((1,n)\\in L_n\\).\n\n* For \\(2\\le i\\le n-1\\) (including the special replacement when\n  \\(i=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) and \\(n\\) odd) the line \\(L_i\\) has equation  \n\n  \\[\n  y-i = \\frac{n+1-2i}{i-1}\\,(x-1).\n  \\tag{11}\n  \\]\n\n  Because \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(b=i\\), we have  \n\n  \\[\n  b-i = 0 = \\frac{n+1-2i}{i-1}\\,(a-1) .\n  \\tag{12}\n  \\]\n\n  The factor \\(\\frac{n+1-2i}{i-1}\\) is non\u2011zero (by the discussion above);\n  consequently (12) forces \\(a=1\\).  The only points of \\(S_n\\) with second\n  coordinate \\(i\\) and first coordinate \\(1\\) are precisely \\((1,i)\\), which\n  lies on \\(L_i\\) by construction.  Hence every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to the\n  line indexed by its second coordinate.\n\nThus the family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines\nand covers the whole set \\(S_n\\).  Consequently \\(k=n\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Impossibility of intermediate values\n\n#### Lemma 2.1 (left\u2011column bound)\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\nC_n=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) points.  Any line that is **not** vertical can contain at most\none of these points, because two distinct points with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate\nwould force the line to be vertical.  Hence covering \\(C_n\\) with lines that are\nnot vertical requires at least \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n#### Lemma 2.2 (a non\u2011sunny line forces an extra line)\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of distinct lines covers \\(S_n\\) and contains a\nnon\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell\\).\n\n*If \\(\\ell\\) is horizontal*, say \\(\\ell\\colon y=h\\) with \\(1\\le h\\le n-1\\):\nthe points of the left column that lie on \\(\\ell\\) are  \n\n\\[\nR_h=\\{(1,h)\\}.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nAll other points of the left column \\(C_n\\setminus R_h\\) must be covered by\nlines that are not horizontal; by Lemma\u202f2.1 each such line can contain at most\none of them, so at least \\(|C_n|-1 = n-1\\) additional lines are needed.\nTogether with the horizontal line \\(\\ell\\) we already have \\(n\\) lines.\nNow look at the points of the bottom row  \n\n\\[\nB_h=\\{(a,h)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-h\\},\n\\qquad |B_h|=n+1-h\\ge2 .\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nA sunny line can meet the horizontal line \\(y=h\\) in at most one lattice\npoint; consequently the points of \\(B_h\\) require at least \\(n+1-h\\) distinct\nlines, one of which is \\(\\ell\\) itself.  Hence the total number of lines is at\nleast  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n+1-h)=n+h\\ge n+1 .\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(\\ell\\) is vertical* the same argument is applied after rotating the\npicture by \\(90^{\\circ}\\); the conclusion is again that at least \\(n+1\\) lines\nare necessary.\n\n*If \\(\\ell\\) has slope \\(-1\\)*, then \\(\\ell\\) contains all points of the\nhypotenuse \\(\\{(i,n+1-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\).  The left column points that are\nnot on \\(\\ell\\) are \\((1,b)\\) with \\(b\\neq n+1-1 = n\\); there are \\(n-1\\) of\nthem and, as before, each requires a distinct non\u2011vertical line.  Adding the\n\\(-1\\) line itself gives at least \\(n\\) lines, and the same counting of the\nbottom row as above yields one more, for a total of at least \\(n+1\\).\n\nThus **any** covering of \\(S_n\\) that contains a non\u2011sunny line needs at least\n\\(n+1\\) distinct lines.\n\n#### Corollary 2.3\n\nA covering of \\(S_n\\) by exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines cannot contain a\nnon\u2011sunny line.  Hence, for a covering with \\(n\\) lines, either all \\(n\\) lines\nare sunny (\\(k=n\\)) or none of them is sunny (\\(k=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Final conclusion\n\nThe two explicit families constructed in Section\u202f1 show that the values\n\\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) are realizable for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).\nCorollary\u202f2.3 proves that no covering with exactly \\(n\\) lines can contain a\nmixture of sunny and non\u2011sunny lines; consequently no intermediate value\n\\(0<k<n\\) is possible.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge3\\text{ the admissible numbers of sunny lines are }\nk=0\\text{ and }k=n.}\n\\]"
