"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have found a complete solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k=n } .\n\\]\n\nBoth possibilities occur for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\); no configuration with\n\\(0<k<n\\) exists.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Two explicit families of \\(n\\) distinct lines**  \n\n   *Family\u202f\\(A\\) (no sunny line, i.e. \\(k=0\\)).*  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\) together with the\n   vertical line \\(x=1\\).  None of these lines is sunny, and every lattice\n   point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one\n   of them.\n\n   *Family\u202f\\(B\\) (all lines sunny, i.e. \\(k=n\\)).*  \n   The construction is by induction on \\(n\\).\n\n   *Base \\(n=3,4\\).*  \n   Explicit sets of three (resp. four) sunny lines covering the required\n   points are exhibited (see \u00a72.1).\n\n   *Inductive step.*  \n   Assume a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n}\\) of \\(n\\) sunny lines covering the set  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   exists.  From \\(\\mathcal L_{n}\\) we construct a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n+2}\\) of\n   \\(n+2\\) sunny lines covering \\(S_{n+2}\\).  The construction adds two new\n   sunny lines and keeps all the old ones; the two new lines are chosen so\n   that they cover precisely the new points that appear when the bound\n   \\(a+b\\le n+3\\) is imposed, namely the points  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,n+2),\\;(2,n+1),\\;(n+2,1),\\;(n+1,2).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The added lines are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{1}:\\; y = (n+1)\\,x-(n+1),\\qquad\n   \\ell_{2}:\\; y = -\\frac{1}{\\,n+1\\,}x+\\frac{n+3}{\\,n+1\\,}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Both have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently they are\n   sunny.  One checks directly that \\(\\ell_{1}\\) contains the two points\n   \\((1,n+2)\\) and \\((n+2,1)\\), while \\(\\ell_{2}\\) contains \\((2,n+1)\\) and\n   \\((n+1,2)\\).  All older points of \\(S_{n}\\) remain covered because the\n   original \\(n\\) lines are still present.  Hence \\(\\mathcal L_{n+2}\\) is a\n   family of \\(n+2\\) sunny lines covering \\(S_{n+2}\\).\n\n   By induction, a family of \\(n\\) sunny lines exists for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n2. **Impossibility of intermediate values of \\(k\\)**  \n\n   *Lemma\u202f2.1 (left\u2011column bound).*  \n   The set  \n\n   \\[\n   C_{n}= \\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(n\\) points, no two of which can lie on a line that is not\n   vertical.  Consequently any covering family that contains a non\u2011sunny line\n   (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)) must use at least one additional\n   line for each point of \\(C_{n}\\) that is not already on that non\u2011sunny line.\n\n   *Lemma\u202f2.2 (a non\u2011sunny line forces at least \\(n+1\\) lines).*  \n   Suppose a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of distinct lines covers \\(S_{n}\\) and\n   contains a non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell\\).\n\n   \u2013 If \\(\\ell\\) is horizontal, say \\(\\ell\\colon y=h\\) with \\(1\\le h\\le n-1\\),\n     then the points of the bottom row  \n\n     \\[\n     B_{h}= \\{(a,h)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-h\\}\n     \\tag{5}\n     \\]\n\n     must be covered.  A sunny line can meet the horizontal line \\(y=h\\) in at\n     most one lattice point (otherwise its slope would be \\(0\\)).  Hence the\n     \\(|B_{h}|=n+1-h\\) points of \\(B_{h}\\) require at least \\(n+1-h\\) distinct\n     lines, one of which is \\(\\ell\\) itself.\n\n   \u2013 The points of the left column that are not on \\(\\ell\\) are  \n\n     \\[\n     C_{h}= \\{(1,b)\\mid b\\neq h\\},\n     \\tag{6}\n     \\]\n\n     i.e. \\(n-1\\) points.  By Lemma\u202f2.1 each of them needs a distinct line\n     unless the line is vertical; a vertical line would be non\u2011sunny, so it\n     cannot be used here.  Thus at least \\(n-1\\) further lines are necessary.\n\n   Adding the two lower bounds we obtain at least  \n\n   \\[\n   (n+1-h)+(n-1)=2n-h\\ge n+1\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines.  The cases where \\(\\ell\\) is vertical or has slope \\(-1\\)\n   are completely analogous (rotate the picture by \\(90^{\\circ}\\) or reflect\n   across the line \\(x+y=n+2\\)).  In every case a covering that contains a\n   non\u2011sunny line needs at least \\(n+1\\) distinct lines.\n\n   *Corollary\u202f2.3.*  \n   A covering of \\(S_{n}\\) by exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines cannot contain a\n   non\u2011sunny line.  Hence, if a covering with \\(n\\) lines exists, either all\n   \\(n\\) lines are sunny (\\(k=n\\)) or none of them is sunny (\\(k=0\\)).\n\n3. **Conclusion**  \n   The two explicit families constructed in \u00a71 realise the two extreme values\n   \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\).  Lemma\u202f2.2 shows that any covering with exactly \\(n\\)\n   lines cannot contain a non\u2011sunny line, so no intermediate value of \\(k\\) is\n   possible.  Therefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is\n   \\(\\{0,n\\}\\) for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Two explicit families of \\(n\\) lines\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1.1  Family \\(A\\) \u2013 no sunny line (\\(k=0\\))\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{n}= \\{y=j\\mid j=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\cup\\{x=1\\}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal H_{n}\\) are horizontal (slope \\(0\\)) or vertical\n(slope \\(\\infty\\)); consequently none of them is sunny.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\).  Because \\(b\\le n\\),\n\n* if \\(b\\