The set \\(S_{m+2}\\) contains \\(S_{m}\\) and, in addition, the four new points\n\n\\[\n(1,m+1),\\;(2,m),\\;(m+1,1),\\;(m,2).\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nAdd to \\(\\mathcal L_{m}\\) the two lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ell_{a}&:\\; y = (m+1)\\,x-(m+1),\\\\\n\\ell_{b}&:\\; y = -\\frac{1}{\\,m+1\\,}x+\\frac{m+3}{\\,m+1\\,}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nBoth slopes, \\(m+1\\) and \\(-\\dfrac{1}{m+1}\\), are different from\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore \\(\\ell_{a}\\) and \\(\\ell_{b}\\) are sunny.\n\nA direct substitution shows\n\n\\[\n\\ell_{a}\\; \\text{contains}\\; (1,m+1)\\ \\text{and}\\ (m+1,1),\\qquad\n\\ell_{b}\\; \\text{contains}\\; (2,m)\\ \\text{and}\\ (m,2).\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nConsequently every point of \\(S_{m+2}\\) is covered by a line of\n\\(\\mathcal L_{m}\\cup\\{\\ell_{a},\\ell_{b}\\}\\).  The resulting family has\n\\(|\\mathcal L_{m}|+2 = m+2\\) distinct sunny lines, completing the\ninduction.\n\nBecause the induction starts at the odd base \\(n=3\\), it yields a family of\n\\(n\\) sunny lines for every odd \\(n\\ge 3\\).  For an even integer \\(n\\ge 4\\)\nwrite \\(n= (n-1)+1\\); apply the construction for the odd number \\(n-1\\) to\nobtain a family of \\(n-1\\) sunny lines covering \\(S_{n-1}\\), and then add the\nsingle sunny line \\(y = (n-1)x-(n-1)\\), which contains the two new points\n\\((1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\\).  The resulting family consists of \\(n\\) sunny lines\nand covers \\(S_{n}\\).\n\nThus for **every** integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct\nsunny lines covering \\(S_{n}\\); i.e. the value \\(k=n\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  No intermediate values of \\(k\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f2.1 (left\u2011column bound)\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\nC_{n}= \\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) points.  Any line that is not vertical can contain at most one\nof t