al H_{n}\\)) contains the point.  \n  * If \\(b=n\\) then the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a=1\\); the vertical line \\(x=1\\) contains \\((1,n)\\).  \n\nThus every point of \\(S_{n}\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal H_{n}\\). All lines are either horizontal (slope\u202f0) or vertical (slope\u202f\\(\\infty\\)), so none is sunny. The family has exactly \\(n\\) lines ( \\(n-1\\) horizontals + 1 vertical).  \n\n**Conclusion.** This part is correct; it establishes that \\(k=0\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2. Family\u202f\\(B\\) \u2013 \\(k=n\\) (Section\u202f2.1.2)\n\n#### 2.1 Base Cases \\(n=3,4\\)\n\n*For \\(n=3\\)* the three lines listed in (10) are indeed sunny (slopes \\(2,-2,1\\)) and each passes through one of the three points of \\(S_{3}\\).  \n\n*For \\(n=4\\)* the four lines in (11) have slopes \\(1,1, -2,1\\). The line \\(y=x\\) is sunny (slope\u202f1), \\(y=x+1\\) is sunny, \\(y=-2x+5\\) is sunny, and \\(y=x-2\\) is sunny. Each point of \\(S_{4}\\) is covered as claimed.  \n\nBoth base cases are correct.\n\n#### 2.2 Inductive Step (odd \\(m\\to m+2\\))\n\nThe solution assumes a family \\(\\mathcal L_{m}\\) of \\(m\\) sunny lines covering \\(S_{m}\\) and attempts to extend it to a family \\(\\mathcal L_{m+2}\\) of \\(m+2\\) sunny lines covering \\(S_{m+2}\\) by adding the two lines\n\n\\[\n\\ell_{a}: y = (m+1)x-(m+1),\\qquad\n\\ell_{b}: y = -\\frac{1}{m+1}x+\\frac{m+3}{m+1}.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThe four *new* points that appear in \\(S_{m+2}\\) but not in \\(S_{m}\\) are\n\n\\[\n(1,m+1),\\;(2,m),\\;(m+1,1),\\;(m,2). \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThe author asserts (14) that \\(\\ell_{a}\\) contains \\((1,m+1)\\) and \\((m+1,1)\\), while \\(\\ell_{b}\\) contains \\((2,m)\\) and \\((m,2)\\).\n\n**Direct substitution check**\n\n*For \\(\\ell_{a}\\):*  \n- At \\((1,m+1)\\): RHS \\(=(m+1)\\cdot1-(m+1)=0\\neq m+1\\).  \n- At \\((m+1,1)\\): RHS \\(=(m+1)(m+1)-(m+1)=m(m+1)\\neq1\\).\n\n*For \\(\\ell_{b}\\):*  \n- At \\((2,m)\\): RHS \\(-\\frac{