ring \\(S_{m+2}\\).\n\nThe set \\(S_{m+2}\\) contains \\(S_{m}\\) and, in addition, the four new points\n\n\\[\n(1,m+1),\\;(2,m),\\;(m+1,1),\\;(m,2).\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nAdd to \\(\\mathcal L_{m}\\) the two lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ell_{a}&:\\; y = (m+1)\\,x-(m+1),\\\\\n\\ell_{b}&:\\; y = -\\frac{1}{\\,m+1\\,}x+\\frac{m+3}{\\,m+1\\,}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nBoth slopes, \\(m+1\\) and \\(-\\dfrac{1}{m+1}\\), are different from\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore \\(\\ell_{a}\\) and \\(\\ell_{b}\\) are sunny.\n\nA direct substitution shows\n\n\\[\n\\ell_{a}\\; \\text{contains}\\; (1,m+1)\\ \\text{and}\\ (m+1,1),\\qquad\n\\ell_{b}\\; \\text{contains}\\; (2,m)\\ \\text{and}\\ (m,2).\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nConsequently every point of \\(S_{m+2}\\) is covered by a line of\n\\(\\mathcal L_{m}\\cup\\{\\ell_{a},\\ell_{b}\\}\\).  The resulting family has\n\\(|\\mathcal L_{m}|+2 = m+2\\) distinct sunny lines, completing the\ninduction.\n\nBecause the induction starts at the odd base \\(n=3\\), it yields a family of\n\\(n\\) sunny lines for every odd \\(n\\ge 3\\).  For an even integer \\(n\\ge 4\\)\nwrite \\(n= (n-1)+1\\); apply the construction for the odd number \\(n-1\\) to\nobtain a family of \\(n-1\\) sunny lines covering \\(S_{n-1}\\), and then add the\nsingle sunny line \\(y = (n-1)x-(n-1)\\), which contains the two new points\n\\((1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\\).  The resulting family consists of \\(n\\) sunny lines\nand covers \\(S_{n}\\).\n\nThus for **every** integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct\nsunny lines covering \\(S_{n}\\); i.e. the value \\(k=n\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  No intermediate values of \\(k\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f2.1 (left\u2011column bound)\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\nC_{n}= \\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) points.  Any line that is not vertical can contain at most one\nof these points, because two distinct points with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate\nforce the line to be vertical.  Hence covering \\(C_{n}\\) with lines that are\nnot vertical requires at least \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f2.2 (a non\u2011sunny line forces at least \\(n+1\\) lines)\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of distinct lines covers \\(S_{n}\\) and contains\na non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell\\).\n\n*If \\(\\ell\\) is horizontal*, say \\(\\ell\\colon y=h\\) with \\(1\\le h\\le n-1\\),\nthen the points of the bottom row  \n\n\\[\nB_{h}= \\{(a,h)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-h\\}\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nmust be covered.  A sunny line can intersect the horizontal line \\(y=h\\) in at\nmost one lattice point; otherwise its slope would be \\(0\\).  Consequently\n\\(|B_{h}|=n+1-h\\) distinct lines are needed, one of which is \\(\\ell\\) itself.\n\nThe points of the left column that are not on \\(\\ell\\) are  \n\n\\[\nC_{h}= \\{(1,b)\\mid b\\neq h\\},\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\(n-1\\) points.  By Lemma\u202f2.1 each of them needs a distinct line unless\nthe line is vertical, but a vertical line would be non\u2011sunny, contradicting\nthe assumption that \\(\\ell\\) is the *only* non\u2011sunny line.  Hence at least\n\\(n-1\\) further distinct lines are required.\n\nAltogether we need at least  \n\n\\[\n(n+1-h)+(n-1)=2n-h\\ge n+1\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\ndifferent lines.\n\n*If \\(\\ell\\) is vertical* or has slope \\(-1\\), the same counting argument\napplies after rotating the picture by \\(90^{\\circ}\\) or reflecting it across the\nline \\(x+y=n+2\\).  In every case a covering that contains a non\u2011sunny line\nneeds at least \\(n+1\\) distinct lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Corollary\u202f2.3  \n\nA covering of \\(S_{n}\\) by exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines cannot contain a\nnon\u2011sunny line.  Therefore any covering with \\(n\\) lines has either\n\n* all \\(n\\) lines sunny (\\(k=n\\)), or\n* none of the \\(n\\) lines sunny (\\(k=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Final conclusion\n\nThe two families constructed in \u00a72.1 show that the extreme values\n\\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) are indeed attainable for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\nLemma\u202f2.2 proves that a covering with exactly \\(n\\) lines cannot contain a\nnon\u2011sunny line, and Lemma\u202f2.1 rules out any mixture of sunny and\nnon\u2011sunny lines.  Consequently no intermediate value \\(0<k<n\\) can occur.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge 3\\text{ the admissible numbers of sunny lines are }\nk=0\\text{ and }k=n.}\n\\]"
